camera_and_editing:
Shape match cuts (Bass grammar):
- the six title bars into six glasses;
- the wine ring into the coaster, and later into the blood pool;
- the card rectangle into the phone screen, the oven door and the back door;
- the line of light under the bathroom door into the line of gas haze;
- Angelo's failed spark into the first fire window;
- the title bars into the strobing fence slats at the end.

feasibility_and_risks:
THE BIG HEDGE. This direction can be built entirely in Canvas 2D, and silhouette is the strongest available answer to the brief's biggest risk, procedurally drawn faces. There are no faces to get wrong. Faces exist only as profiles and as Bass-style graphic eyes in at most 8 extreme close-ups. Silhouettes also merge where limbs overlap, so simple rigs read as seamless.

WHAT IS GENUINELY HARD:

1. Hands. The whole film is built on hands: crisps, dealing, the wrist grab, the hand circle, the shoulder squeeze, the clasp, the knife, the lighter, the final two hands. They need a hand-authored library of about 16 high-quality SVG silhouettes, drawn at 4x size:
- open, relaxed, card pinch, deal, point;
- wrist grip, left clasp, right clasp;
- covering the throat, limp palm-up, knife grip, lighter thumb;
- shoulder squeeze, slap-flat, tremble-on-shoulder;
- the Extra's right hand.
This is where most of the craft budget should go.

2. Walking and turning. Walks happen only in long shots and in depth, drawn on twos. Turns happen across cuts, as in Reiniger, and a figure is never rotated in 3D.

3. The hand-circle top-down: four linked arm chains plus an empty fifth corner. Build it as one bespoke, hand-tuned composition, not a general IK solve.

4. Fire and explosion. Particle soup would look cheap. Flat three-tone animated flame shapes, ember particles and the negative-inversion frames look designed rather than simulated.

5. The type engine is real software:
- per-glyph layout using measureText per character, with pair kerning;
- eight behaviour classes;
- set-plane transforms and depth sorting;
- knockout compositing.
Fonts must be force-loaded with the FontFace API before frame 0, or exports will silently fall back to other fonts.

6. Export performance. ctx.filter blur (halation, background defocus) is expensive at 1080p, so cache static blurred layers per shot. Rendering all 20,160 frames headless will likely take 0.1-0.3 s per frame, so 1-2 hours per full export. ctx.filter only works in Chromium and Firefox, so the scrub preview targets Chrome.

7. Readability. With no voice actors, the type is the performance for 14 minutes. Enforce the hold rule (max(0.8 s, 0.3 s + 55 ms per character)), allow at most three active lines, and test-read the assembled cut at full speed before polishing.

8. Telling speakers apart in group silhouettes. Four redundant cues handle it: typeface, colour, placement next to the speaker, and landing sound.

9. Warmth in Act 1. Silhouettes can feel cold. The first act's charm comes from comic timing on twos, Christof's marquee lines, Angelo's dealt words, Kay's lingering lip-mark letters, and the warmest palette in the film.

10. The Extra must stay ambiguous. The visibility ladder needs disciplined review: if any frame gives it an outline or an eye, the film breaks.

HOW IT LOOKS PREMIUM RATHER THAN CHEAP:
- strict compositional grids and symmetry, in the Bass tradition;
- posterised three-band light instead of soft gradients;
- cut-paper edge noise, frozen so edges are still until we choose to let them boil;
- rim-light crescents;
- halation on highlights, seeded grain, gate weave and a paper-fibre texture;
- motion on twos with real anticipation and overshoot;
- at most four colours per shot;
- sound doing at least half the work.

VIOLENCE stays implication: it is shown through shape (curls squared off against a wall, a ruler-straight line of light), shadow (the body projected on the ceiling) and colour (wine-dark pools). Each image is held under a second the first time it is seen. It hits hard because it is so clean, and it is never gratuitous.
